    editorialDisclosure: "No brand-supplied product or sponsored testing applied to this review unless stated. Affiliate availability does not affect scores or verdict.",
    sections: [{"id":"sec-overview","heading":"What it is","body":"Functional fitness trainer aimed at CrossFit-style and HYROX station work.\n\nBear KompleX Valor is built for a specific job in the category — use that job as your first filter.\n\nI'd shortlist it when you want stable for lifts and stations or hYROX-friendly design brief.\n\nI'd pause if narrower retail availability in EU would show up often in your week.\n\nBottom line: Functional fitness trainer aimed at CrossFit-style and HYROX station work. I'd shortlist it when you want stable for lifts and stations. I'd pause if narrower retail availability in EU shows up often in your week.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-verified-specs","heading":"Key specs","body":"Here are the numbers that matter for the Bear KompleX Valor:\n\n• Stability: stable\n• HYROX Suitability: high\n• Lifting Suitability: high\n• Running Suitability: moderate\n• Gender: unisex\n\nWeight and stack shape how protective or lively a shoe feels; drop and geometry change the transition; stability and terrain labels tell you the intended job; width options change who can wear it. For watches and other gear, treat battery, sensors and materials the same way — as filters, not the whole story.\n\nSpecs will not tell you blister risk, late-run foam feel or whether a guidance system suits your gait. Try before you commit when you can, or buy somewhere returns are easy.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-fit","heading":"Fit & Comfort","body":"If you already run in Bear KompleX dailies, start with your usual length in the Bear KompleX Valor and judge width and volume on an easy jog — not standing still in the shop.\n\nIf you sit between sizes or need a wide last, try in store or buy somewhere returns are easy.\n\nNeutral last — no medial post. It suits runners who already like neutrals and want comfort for easy/long road miles, not race lockdown.\n\nBest fit match: average-volume feet on easy and recovery days. Less ideal: wide forefeet needing 2E/4E, very high insteps that fight a snug midfoot, or anyone shopping one shoe for both easy miles and race-day snugness.\n\nFit caveats to take seriously: narrower retail availability in EU.\n\nWear the socks you train in. If heel slip or hot spots show up in the first easy session, change size/width or last — do not hope break-in will rewrite the shoe.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-cushioning","heading":"Cushioning","body":"Cushioning on the Bear KompleX Valor is tuned for its daily role first. Match that lane to your week before you buy on foam names alone.\n\nIf your week is mostly easy miles, prioritise protection. If your week is workouts and races, a firmer or plated shoe usually fits better.\n\nHeel strikers and runners who want soft landings on tired legs usually like this brief. Forefoot strikers who want ground feel, and anyone doing weekly speed work in one shoe, often will not.\n\nNew to tall soft stacks? Use easy days first. The platform can feel tippier or less precise until your legs adapt — then decide if the plushness is worth it for your long runs.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-ride","heading":"Ride","body":"How the Bear KompleX Valor rides should match the sessions you will wear it for most weeks.\n\nThat lines up with its main job: stable for lifts and stations.\n\nMatch the ride to your week. Crossing lanes without a reason is how people end up with a closet of almost-right shoes.\n\nWhere the ride frustrates people: narrower retail availability in EU.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-stability","heading":"Stability","body":"The Bear KompleX Valor is neutral — no guidance post or medial rail. It suits runners who already run happily in neutrals and want this shoe’s daily role.\n\nIf a coach or clinician has you in guidance shoes, this is the wrong lane — look at Kayano, Adrenaline or Structure-class peers.\n\nFoot and style fit: average-volume neutral strikers on road easy days. Less ideal: runners who need medial support, wide feet in a standard-only width, or anyone who feels insecure on tall soft stacks at tired-end long-run pace.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-upper","heading":"Upper & lockdown","body":"Treat the upper as lockdown and breathability — try it laced for an easy run, not just standing in the shop.\n\nLockdown check: heel should stay put on strides, tongue should not dig, and the midfoot should feel secure without hot spots after 20–30 easy minutes. Race-snug is the wrong target for a soft daily.\n\nIf heel slip or hot spots show up in the first easy session, size/width is wrong or this last is not for you — do not hope break-in will rewrite the upper.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-grip","heading":"Grip & outsole","body":"The Bear KompleX Valor wears a road outsole — pavement, bike paths, sidewalks and treadmill. It is not a trail shoe, and deep mud or wet roots will show that quickly.\n\nOn wet painted lines, metal covers and early-morning damp asphalt, road rubber is about reliable contact — not aggressive lugs. If most of your miles are soft singletrack, buy a trail shoe instead of wishing this one had bite.\n\nFor durability of grip, watch the heel and forefoot pods on abrasive city routes. Once the rubber is shiny and the shoe feels skittish on wet paint, it is time to retire or rotate — not when the upper still looks new.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-durability","heading":"Durability","body":"I'd expect the Bear KompleX Valor to hold up like a solid daily in its class if you stick to the surfaces it was built for.\n\nRoad rubber is for pavement and treadmill. Rough concrete and grit chew heel and forefoot pods faster than soft paths — watch those contact patches, and retire the shoe when the ride goes dead, not when the upper still looks new.\n\nPractical plan: pair soft easy miles here with a firmer daily for mixed weeks. That is usually how you get good value and consistent feel from a plush shoe.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-strengths","heading":"Where it is strongest","body":"I'd buy the Bear KompleX Valor when these outcomes match what you need most weeks:\n\nWhat it does well\n• Stable for lifts and stations\n• HYROX-friendly design brief\n\nThe standout is stable for lifts and stations. If that is your main weekly need, keep going. If not, check trade-offs and alternatives.\n\nBest for: You want stable for lifts and stations as a weekly priority — that is the Valor's main job; Most of your sessions line up with hYROX-friendly design brief more than an adjacent specialty role.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]}],
    pros: ["Stable for lifts and stations","HYROX-friendly design brief","Functional fitness trainer aimed at CrossFit-style and HYROX station work"],
    cons: ["Narrower retail availability in EU"],
    whoShouldBuy: ["You want stable for lifts and stations as a weekly priority — that is the Valor's main job","Most of your sessions line up with hYROX-friendly design brief more than an adjacent specialty role"],
    whoShouldAvoid: ["You need to avoid narrower retail availability in EU — look at Trainer or a clearer specialist instead of forcing the Valor","You want one shoe for every session and pace — the Valor is a defined role; Trainer or a second shoe may cover the gaps better","Your fit, surface, or support needs sit outside what Bear KompleX built the Valor to do"],
