    sections: [{"id":"sec-overview","heading":"What it is","body":"Budget MIP running watch focused on simple GPS tracking and beginner coaching.\n\nGarmin Forerunner 55 is built for a specific job in the category — use that job as your first filter.\n\nI'd shortlist it when you want lowest-cost Garmin runner entry or long MIP battery.\n\nI'd pause if dated display and feature set or limited multisport depth would show up often in your week.\n\nBottom line: Budget MIP running watch focused on simple GPS tracking and beginner coaching. I'd shortlist it when you want lowest-cost Garmin runner entry. I'd pause if dated display and feature set shows up often in your week.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-verified-specs","heading":"Key specs","body":"Here are the numbers that matter for the Garmin Forerunner 55:\n\n• Display Type: mip\n• Touchscreen: No\n• Multi-band GPS: No\n• Maps: No\n• Navigation: No\n• Music: No\n• Payments: No\n• Heart Rate: Yes\n• ECG: No\n• SpO2: No\n• Running Dynamics: No\n• Training Readiness: No\n\nWeight and stack shape how protective or lively a shoe feels; drop and geometry change the transition; stability and terrain labels tell you the intended job; width options change who can wear it. For watches and other gear, treat battery, sensors and materials the same way — as filters, not the whole story.\n\nSpecs will not tell you blister risk, late-run foam feel or whether a guidance system suits your gait. Try before you commit when you can, or buy somewhere returns are easy.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-tech","heading":"Tracking & features","body":"Here's what the Garmin Forerunner 55 offers on the feature sheet, based on published specs and what is normal in this category.\n\nBudget MIP running watch focused on simple GPS tracking and beginner coaching.\n\nPrioritise the capabilities you will use every week. Spec sheets are easy to overbuy — battery, GNSS, maps, sensors and smart notifications only matter if they change how you train or recover.\n\nFeature strengths that matter in practice: Lowest-cost Garmin runner entry; Long MIP battery.\n\nFeature trade-offs to weigh: Dated display and feature set; Limited multisport depth.\n\nBattery claims assume a usage profile. Always-on displays, music storage, multi-band GNSS and cold weather all move real-world numbers. If you care about multi-day adventures, size the watch by your worst-case activity mix, not the marketing headline.\n\nEcosystem lock-in is part of value: phone OS compatibility, coach platforms, and whether you already own heart-rate straps or sensors. Switching brands has a switching cost beyond the sticker price.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-performance","heading":"Everyday performance","body":"Budget MIP running watch focused on simple GPS tracking and beginner coaching.\n\nPerformance for the Garmin Forerunner 55 should be read through intended use. Category-leading numbers in the wrong session still feel like a miss.\n\nWhere it is set up to perform\n• Lowest-cost Garmin runner entry\n• Long MIP battery\n\nWhere it is less convincing\n• Dated display and feature set\n• Limited multisport depth\n\nBest performance fit: You want lowest-cost Garmin runner entry as a weekly priority — that is the Forerunner 55's main job; Most of your sessions line up with long MIP battery more than an adjacent specialty role.\n\nWeaker performance fit: You need to avoid dated display and feature set — forcing the Forerunner 55 into that job usually disappoints; You need to avoid limited multisport depth on this model; You need to avoid no full offline maps on this model.\n\nJudge it for the sessions it was built for. A race shoe will “win” a sprint feel test that a daily trainer was never meant to take — that does not make the trainer bad.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-strengths","heading":"Where it is strongest","body":"I'd buy the Garmin Forerunner 55 when these outcomes match what you need most weeks:\n\nWhat it does well\n• Lowest-cost Garmin runner entry\n• Long MIP battery\n\nThe standout is lowest-cost Garmin runner entry. If that is your main weekly need, keep going. If not, check trade-offs and alternatives.\n\nBest for: You want lowest-cost Garmin runner entry as a weekly priority — that is the Forerunner 55's main job; Most of your sessions line up with long MIP battery more than an adjacent specialty role.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-tradeoffs","heading":"Trade-offs & limits","body":"The Garmin Forerunner 55 is not trying to do every job. These are the compromises to weigh:\n\n• Dated display and feature set\n• Limited multisport depth\n\nSkip it if: You need to avoid dated display and feature set — forcing the Forerunner 55 into that job usually disappoints; You need to avoid limited multisport depth on this model; You need to avoid no full offline maps on this model.\n\nThose limits matter when they hit your primary sessions — not when they only show up once a month. If your week sits outside this product's job, pick a peer built for that job instead.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-usecase","heading":"Who it is for","body":"I'd buy the Garmin Forerunner 55 when most of your weekly sessions match what it does well.\n\nBest when you want\n• You want lowest-cost Garmin runner entry as a weekly priority — that is the Forerunner 55's main job\n• Most of your sessions line up with long MIP battery more than an adjacent specialty role\n• Lowest-cost Garmin runner entry\n• Long MIP battery\n\nLook elsewhere when\n• You need to avoid dated display and feature set — forcing the Forerunner 55 into that job usually disappoints\n• You need to avoid limited multisport depth on this model\n• You need to avoid no full offline maps on this model\n• Dated display and feature set\n• Limited multisport depth\n\nIf more than half your week sits in the “look elsewhere” list, open the alternatives instead of forcing a compromise.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-value","heading":"Value","body":"You are mainly paying for lowest-cost Garmin runner entry or long MIP battery.\n\nYou are also accepting dated display and feature set or limited multisport depth — fine when that is not your weekly mix.\n\nStrongest value for: You want lowest-cost Garmin runner entry as a weekly priority — that is the Forerunner 55's main job; Most of your sessions line up with long MIP battery more than an adjacent specialty role.\n\nWeaker value for: You need to avoid dated display and feature set — forcing the Forerunner 55 into that job usually disappoints; You need to avoid limited multisport depth on this model; You need to avoid no full offline maps on this model.\n\nCheck live street price in the offers module. I'd pay the premium when the role is clear; otherwise compare the linked alternatives or a prior generation on sale.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]}],
