    sections: [{"id":"sec-overview","heading":"What it is","body":"Nox ML10 Hexa court shoe with a hexagonal traction pattern for padel movement patterns.\n\nNox ML10 Hexa is built for a specific job in the category — use that job as your first filter.\n\nI'd shortlist it when you want distinct traction, club comfort or nox ecosystem.\n\nI'd pause if less widely stocked than ASICS would show up often in your week.\n\nBottom line: Nox ML10 Hexa court shoe with a hexagonal traction pattern for padel movement patterns. I'd shortlist it when you want distinct traction. I'd pause if less widely stocked than ASICS shows up often in your week.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-verified-specs","heading":"Key specs","body":"Here are the numbers that matter for the Nox ML10 Hexa:\n\n• Outsole: hexa padel\n• Gender: unisex\n• Cushioning: moderate\n• Support: stability\n\nWeight and stack shape how protective or lively a shoe feels; drop and geometry change the transition; stability and terrain labels tell you the intended job; width options change who can wear it. For watches and other gear, treat battery, sensors and materials the same way — as filters, not the whole story.\n\nSpecs will not tell you blister risk, late-run foam feel or whether a guidance system suits your gait. Try before you commit when you can, or buy somewhere returns are easy.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-fit","heading":"Fit & Comfort","body":"If you already run in Nox dailies, start with your usual length in the Nox ML10 Hexa and judge width and volume on an easy jog — not standing still in the shop.\n\nIf you sit between sizes or need a wide last, try in store or buy somewhere returns are easy.\n\nNeutral last — no medial post. It suits runners who already like neutrals and want comfort for easy/long road miles, not race lockdown.\n\nBest fit match: average-volume feet on easy and recovery days. Less ideal: wide forefeet needing 2E/4E, very high insteps that fight a snug midfoot, or anyone shopping one shoe for both easy miles and race-day snugness.\n\nFit caveats to take seriously: less widely stocked than ASICS.\n\nWear the socks you train in. If heel slip or hot spots show up in the first easy session, change size/width or last — do not hope break-in will rewrite the shoe.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-cushioning","heading":"Cushioning","body":"Cushioning on the Nox ML10 Hexa is tuned for its daily role first. Match that lane to your week before you buy on foam names alone.\n\nIf your week is mostly easy miles, prioritise protection. If your week is workouts and races, a firmer or plated shoe usually fits better.\n\nHeel strikers and runners who want soft landings on tired legs usually like this brief. Forefoot strikers who want ground feel, and anyone doing weekly speed work in one shoe, often will not.\n\nNew to tall soft stacks? Use easy days first. The platform can feel tippier or less precise until your legs adapt — then decide if the plushness is worth it for your long runs.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-ride","heading":"Ride","body":"How the Nox ML10 Hexa rides should match the sessions you will wear it for most weeks.\n\nThat lines up with its main job: distinct traction.\n\nMatch the ride to your week. Crossing lanes without a reason is how people end up with a closet of almost-right shoes.\n\nWhere the ride frustrates people: less widely stocked than ASICS.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-stability","heading":"Stability","body":"The Nox ML10 Hexa is neutral — no guidance post or medial rail. It suits runners who already run happily in neutrals and want this shoe’s daily role.\n\nIf a coach or clinician has you in guidance shoes, this is the wrong lane — look at Kayano, Adrenaline or Structure-class peers.\n\nFoot and style fit: average-volume neutral strikers on road easy days. Less ideal: runners who need medial support, wide feet in a standard-only width, or anyone who feels insecure on tall soft stacks at tired-end long-run pace.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-upper","heading":"Upper & lockdown","body":"Treat the upper as lockdown and breathability — try it laced for an easy run, not just standing in the shop.\n\nLockdown check: heel should stay put on strides, tongue should not dig, and the midfoot should feel secure without hot spots after 20–30 easy minutes. Race-snug is the wrong target for a soft daily.\n\nIf heel slip or hot spots show up in the first easy session, size/width is wrong or this last is not for you — do not hope break-in will rewrite the upper.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-grip","heading":"Grip & outsole","body":"The Nox ML10 Hexa wears a road outsole — pavement, bike paths, sidewalks and treadmill. It is not a trail shoe, and deep mud or wet roots will show that quickly.\n\nOutsole build: hexa padel.\n\nOn wet painted lines, metal covers and early-morning damp asphalt, road rubber is about reliable contact — not aggressive lugs. If most of your miles are soft singletrack, buy a trail shoe instead of wishing this one had bite.\n\nWhere this outsole helps: distinct traction.\n\nFor durability of grip, watch the heel and forefoot pods on abrasive city routes. Once the rubber is shiny and the shoe feels skittish on wet paint, it is time to retire or rotate — not when the upper still looks new.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-durability","heading":"Durability","body":"I'd expect the Nox ML10 Hexa to hold up like a solid daily in its class if you stick to the surfaces it was built for.\n\nRoad rubber is for pavement and treadmill. Rough concrete and grit chew heel and forefoot pods faster than soft paths — watch those contact patches, and retire the shoe when the ride goes dead, not when the upper still looks new.\n\nYou are mostly watching hexa padel contact zones and how protective the midsole still feels late in a long easy run.\n\nPractical plan: pair soft easy miles here with a firmer daily for mixed weeks. That is usually how you get good value and consistent feel from a plush shoe.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-strengths","heading":"Where it is strongest","body":"I'd buy the Nox ML10 Hexa when these outcomes match what you need most weeks:\n\nWhat it does well\n• Distinct traction\n• Club comfort\n• Nox ecosystem\n\nThe standout is distinct traction. If that is your main weekly need, keep going. If not, check trade-offs and alternatives.\n\nBest for: You want distinct traction as a weekly priority — that is the ML10 Hexa's main job; Most of your sessions line up with club comfort more than an adjacent specialty role; Most of your sessions line up with nox ecosystem more than an adjacent specialty role.","evidenceIds":["ev-catalog-mfr","ev-catalog-editorial"]}],
    pros: ["Distinct traction","Club comfort","Nox ecosystem"],
    cons: ["Less widely stocked than ASICS"],
    whoShouldBuy: ["You want distinct traction as a weekly priority — that is the ML10 Hexa's main job","Most of your sessions line up with club comfort more than an adjacent specialty role","Most of your sessions line up with nox ecosystem more than an adjacent specialty role"],
    whoShouldAvoid: ["You need to avoid less widely stocked than ASICS — look at Hybrid Fly or a clearer specialist instead of forcing the ML10 Hexa","You want one shoe for every session and pace — the ML10 Hexa is a defined role; Hybrid Fly or a second shoe may cover the gaps better","Your fit, surface, or support needs sit outside what Nox built the ML10 Hexa to do"],
