    sections: [{"id":"sec-overview","heading":"What it is","body":"Wide barefoot trainer for gym floors, mobility work and natural foot strength.\n\nVivobarefoot Primus Lite III is built for a specific job in the category — use that job as your first filter.\n\nI'd shortlist it when you want wide anatomical fit or thin flexible sole.\n\nI'd pause if not for run-heavy HYROX or max Olympic lifts would show up often in your week.\n\nBottom line: Wide barefoot trainer for gym floors, mobility work and natural foot strength. I'd shortlist it when you want wide anatomical fit. I'd pause if not for run-heavy HYROX or max Olympic lifts shows up often in your week.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-verified-specs","heading":"Key specs","body":"Here are the numbers that matter for the Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III:\n\n• Drop: 0 mm\n• Stability: flexible\n• HYROX Suitability: low\n• Lifting Suitability: moderate\n• Running Suitability: low\n• Gender: unisex\n\nWeight and stack shape how protective or lively a shoe feels; drop and geometry change the transition; stability and terrain labels tell you the intended job; width options change who can wear it. For watches and other gear, treat battery, sensors and materials the same way — as filters, not the whole story.\n\nSpecs will not tell you blister risk, late-run foam feel or whether a guidance system suits your gait. Try before you commit when you can, or buy somewhere returns are easy.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-fit","heading":"Fit & Comfort","body":"If you already run in Vivobarefoot dailies, start with your usual length in the Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III and judge width and volume on an easy jog — not standing still in the shop.\n\nIf you sit between sizes or need a wide last, try in store or buy somewhere returns are easy.\n\nNeutral last — no medial post. It suits runners who already like neutrals and want comfort for easy/long road miles, not race lockdown.\n\nBest fit match: average-volume feet on easy and recovery days. Less ideal: wide forefeet needing 2E/4E, very high insteps that fight a snug midfoot, or anyone shopping one shoe for both easy miles and race-day snugness.\n\nWear the socks you train in. If heel slip or hot spots show up in the first easy session, change size/width or last — do not hope break-in will rewrite the shoe.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-cushioning","heading":"Cushioning","body":"Cushioning on the Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III is tuned for its daily role first. Match that lane to your week before you buy on foam names alone.\n\nOn the numbers: 0 mm drop.\n\nIf your week is mostly easy miles, prioritise protection. If your week is workouts and races, a firmer or plated shoe usually fits better.\n\nHeel strikers and runners who want soft landings on tired legs usually like this brief. Forefoot strikers who want ground feel, and anyone doing weekly speed work in one shoe, often will not.\n\nNew to tall soft stacks? Use easy days first. The platform can feel tippier or less precise until your legs adapt — then decide if the plushness is worth it for your long runs.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-ride","heading":"Ride","body":"How the Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III rides should match the sessions you will wear it for most weeks.\n\nUnderfoot: 0 mm drop shaping the heel-to-toe transition; no plate.\n\nThat lines up with its main job: wide anatomical fit.\n\nMatch the ride to your week. Crossing lanes without a reason is how people end up with a closet of almost-right shoes.\n\nWhere the ride frustrates people: not for run-heavy HYROX or max Olympic lifts.\n\n0 mm drop is familiar for many road runners — it will not feel like a zero-drop shoe, and it will not feel like an aggressive race geometry either.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-stability","heading":"Stability","body":"The Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III is neutral — no guidance post or medial rail. It suits runners who already run happily in neutrals and want this shoe’s daily role.\n\nIf a coach or clinician has you in guidance shoes, this is the wrong lane — look at Kayano, Adrenaline or Structure-class peers.\n\nFoot and style fit: average-volume neutral strikers on road easy days. Less ideal: runners who need medial support, wide feet in a standard-only width, or anyone who feels insecure on tall soft stacks at tired-end long-run pace.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-upper","heading":"Upper & lockdown","body":"Treat the upper as lockdown and breathability — try it laced for an easy run, not just standing in the shop.\n\nLockdown check: heel should stay put on strides, tongue should not dig, and the midfoot should feel secure without hot spots after 20–30 easy minutes. Race-snug is the wrong target for a soft daily.\n\nIf heel slip or hot spots show up in the first easy session, size/width is wrong or this last is not for you — do not hope break-in will rewrite the upper.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-grip","heading":"Grip & outsole","body":"The Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III wears a road outsole — pavement, bike paths, sidewalks and treadmill. It is not a trail shoe, and deep mud or wet roots will show that quickly.\n\nOn wet painted lines, metal covers and early-morning damp asphalt, road rubber is about reliable contact — not aggressive lugs. If most of your miles are soft singletrack, buy a trail shoe instead of wishing this one had bite.\n\nFor durability of grip, watch the heel and forefoot pods on abrasive city routes. Once the rubber is shiny and the shoe feels skittish on wet paint, it is time to retire or rotate — not when the upper still looks new.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-durability","heading":"Durability","body":"I'd expect the Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III to hold up like a solid daily in its class if you stick to the surfaces it was built for.\n\nRoad rubber is for pavement and treadmill. Rough concrete and grit chew heel and forefoot pods faster than soft paths — watch those contact patches, and retire the shoe when the ride goes dead, not when the upper still looks new.\n\nPractical plan: pair soft easy miles here with a firmer daily for mixed weeks. That is usually how you get good value and consistent feel from a plush shoe.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]},{"id":"sec-strengths","heading":"Where it is strongest","body":"I'd buy the Vivobarefoot Primus Lite III when these outcomes match what you need most weeks:\n\nWhat it does well\n• Wide anatomical fit\n• Thin flexible sole\n\nThe standout is wide anatomical fit. If that is your main weekly need, keep going. If not, check trade-offs and alternatives.\n\nBest for: You want wide anatomical fit as a weekly priority — that is the Primus Lite III's main job; Most of your sessions line up with thin flexible sole more than an adjacent specialty role.","evidenceIds":["ev-fitness-mfr","ev-training-shoes-wave-editorial"]}],
    pros: ["Wide anatomical fit","Thin flexible sole","Wide barefoot trainer for gym floors, mobility work and natural foot strength"],
    cons: ["Not for run-heavy HYROX or max Olympic lifts"],
    whoShouldBuy: ["You want wide anatomical fit as a weekly priority — that is the Primus Lite III's main job","Most of your sessions line up with thin flexible sole more than an adjacent specialty role"],
    whoShouldAvoid: ["You need not for run-heavy HYROX or max Olympic lifts — look at Prio or a clearer specialist instead of forcing the Primus Lite III","You want one shoe for every session and pace — the Primus Lite III is a defined role; Prio or a second shoe may cover the gaps better","Your fit, surface, or support needs sit outside what Vivobarefoot built the Primus Lite III to do"],
